        "summary": "When Bonds Stop Behaving Like Bonds",
        "summary_en": "Detailed macroeconomic and sector perspective extracted directly from the reference research publication.",
        "pillars": [
          {
            "title": "核心研报论点",
            "title_en": "Core Thesis",
            "desc": "For nearly four decades, the 60/40 portfolio worked on a simple premise: equities for growth, bonds for ballast. When stocks fell, bonds typically held steady or rallied, cushioning the blow. Comparing portfolio returns from 1986-2020 against 2021-2025 shows that relationship has changed. Pure equity allocations have performed broadly as expected; higher risk, higher return. But every blended allocation tells a different story: adding bonds still reduces expected return as it always has, but it no longer reduces portfolio risk the way it used to. Why? the mechanism is a shift in stock-bond correlation. The correlation was historically negative or near-zero, but has turned positive in this period, blunting the very diversification that bonds were meant to provide.",
            "tag": "逻辑推导"
          },
